From: meta-st-odyssey <build@local>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: stm32mp157c-odyssey: fix PHY MDIO address, add
 reset GPIO and eth-clk-sel

This board's own U-Boot devicetree had the KSZ9031RN at the wrong MDIO
address (0) - the kernel, and the vendor Debian image's boot log, both
confirm it's actually at address 7 (stmmac-0:07).

Also adds:
- phy-reset-gpios, consumed by dwc_eth_qos.c's new
  eqos_start_resets_stm32() (see the driver patch in this same layer).
- st,eth-clk-sel - the property that makes board_interface_eth_init()
  (board/st/stm32mp1/stm32mp1.c) set SYSCFG PMCSETR's eth1_clk_sel bit,
  which actually routes the SoC's 125MHz PLL4_P-derived clock out to
  the PHY as its RGMII reference. Without it (confirmed via "clk dump"
  showing PLL4_P correctly at 125MHz, so the clock itself was never
  the problem - just never routed to the PHY pin), the PHY never gets
  a reference clock at all, and "EQOS_DMA_MODE_SWR stuck" is the
  documented DWC_EQOS behavior when the DMA soft-reset is attempted
  with no live PHY-side clock present - confirmed on hardware via
  "md.l 0x5800b000 1" reading a permanently-stuck 0x00000001,
  unaffected by any amount of extra settle/poll time. The kernel's
  own board DTS has always carried this property; this U-Boot DTS
  (a separate, older/incomplete community port) never did.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [out-of-tree board]

--- a/arch/arm/dts/stm32mp157c-odyssey.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/dts/stm32mp157c-odyssey.dts
@@ -94,13 +94,15 @@
 	phy-mode = "rgmii-id";
 	max-speed = <1000>;
 	phy-handle = <&phy0>;
+	phy-reset-gpios = <&gpiog 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+	st,eth-clk-sel;

 	mdio0 {
 		#address-cells = <1>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
 		compatible = "snps,dwmac-mdio";
-		phy0: ethernet-phy@0 {
-			reg = <0>;
+		phy0: ethernet-phy@7 { /* KSZ9031RN */
+			reg = <7>;
 		};
 	};
 };
